-- Passage rg11-theme-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9f0b915a-6d61-5297-9974-9f87d09224c9', 'rg11-theme-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Unfinished Viola$q$, $q$For forty years, Teodor Vasquez had carved violas in the narrow shop behind his house, and for the last three of them his granddaughter Nadia had swept its floor. When the stiffness in his hands finally made the gouge slip, he set an unfinished viola top on her bench and said nothing about it. She understood the gesture as both a gift and a test.

Nadia opened his notebooks that night. Every thickness was recorded to the tenth of a millimeter, and every arch was traced in pencil so many times that the paper had gone soft. She decided she would make the top exactly as he would have, down to the angle of each cut. For two weeks she measured, carved, tapped the spruce, and measured again. The numbers matched his. The sound did not. When she tapped the plate, it answered with a dull, papery note instead of the bell-like ring she remembered from his instruments.

She brought it to him on a Sunday, expecting him to find her mistake. Teodor held the plate to the window so the light came through the thin places. "You carved my numbers into a different tree," he said. "This spruce grew on a colder slope. It is denser here, softer there. My notebooks describe the wood I had, not the wood you have."

"Then what are the notebooks for?" Nadia asked.

"For learning how to listen," he said. "Not for replacing it."

Over the next month Nadia carved less and tapped more. She thinned the upper edges where the wood resisted and left the center heavier than any of his drawings allowed. Some evenings she wrote her own figures in the margins of his pages, in green ink beside his gray pencil.

When the viola was strung, Teodor played a slow scale and then a second one. He did not say it sounded like his. He said it sounded awake. Nadia kept his notebook open on the bench afterward, but she turned to a blank page at the back and began a list of her own.$q$, 'draft')
