-- Passage rg11-theme-summary-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f762e003-ca09-57c3-a8a2-d833a7afaabe', 'rg11-theme-summary-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Version One$q$, $q$Marisol Etsitty spent her winter break building an app for the Linwood Senior Center. The center ran a volunteer ride program, and every week the director, Mr. Abernathy, sorted dozens of requests for trips to the pharmacy, the clinic, and the grocery store. Marisol's app would handle all of it: a calendar, a map, text reminders, a rating system for drivers, even a chart showing the busiest days of the month.

She presented it in January to a room of twenty residents. She clicked through every screen while they watched politely. When she finished, a woman named Mrs. Kowalczyk raised her hand. "That's very clever," she said. "Where do I write my name?"

By February, four people had used the app. The clipboard by the front desk, a single sheet with lines for name, date, place, and time, was full every week.

Marisol was tempted to blame the residents. Instead she asked Mr. Abernathy if she could sit at the front desk for a few mornings. She watched people stop at the clipboard on their way to coffee, write a line in less than a minute, and chat with whoever stood nearby. Nobody needed a reminder; they reminded each other. The clipboard was not just a sign-up sheet. It was where the day's news traveled.

What Mr. Abernathy did need was help on Sunday nights, when he typed the whole sheet into an email for the drivers and often made mistakes with the times.

Marisol deleted most of what she had built. In its place she wrote a small program that read a photo of the clipboard and turned it into a neat list for the drivers. It had no map, no charts, and no ratings. It took her four days.

The first Sunday it worked, Mr. Abernathy sent her a message with a single line: "Done in five minutes." She printed it and pinned it above her desk, next to the screenshots of the app nobody had used.$q$, 'draft')
